  "response": "The question of whether hereditary information resides in protein or in nucleic acid was settled not by one decisive trial but by a sequence of ever more specific chemical and physiological tests on bacteria and on the viruses that attack them. Griffith's transformation experiments used Streptococcus pneumoniae type II and type III strains injected into mice, and those injections showed that non-virulent pneumococci could be converted into virulent forms, as Frederick Griffith reported in 1928. Griffith published his account of bacterial transformation in 1944. The phenomenon itself demanded a chemical explanation, since something passing from killed cells into living ones had altered a stable character of the recipients.\n\nThe chemical identification of the transforming principle came only after that 1928 report, when Oswald Avery, Colin MacLeod and Maclyn McCarty fractionated pneumococcal extracts and assayed each fraction separately for activity. Deoxyribonuclease treatment destroyed the transforming activity of the Avery group's preparations, and this cannot be squared with the demonstration that transformation of pneumococci was heritable through successive generations of the recipient bacteria; the loss of activity under the enzyme and the stable inheritance of the new character cannot both hold. Enzymatic specificity was the decisive tool in that programme, since proteases and ribonuclease left the activity of the extracts untouched while the purified nucleic acid fraction retained it at very low concentrations.\n\nResistance to the conclusion was chemical rather than merely conservative, and it rested on prevailing views about what kind of molecule a nucleic acid could be. Erwin Chargaff's finding that the amount of adenine equals the amount of thymine in DNA cannot be squared with the fact that Phoebus Levene's tetranucleotide hypothesis led many researchers to regard DNA as too monotonous to encode biological specificity; the equivalence of those two bases and the monotony drawn from the tetranucleotide scheme rule each other out. Settling the dispute therefore required an experimental system in which protein and nucleic acid could be followed separately into a cell and their fates distinguished without recourse to fractionation chemistry at all.\n\nHershey and Chase used carbon-14 to label the phage protein coat, and the distribution of radioactivity between the infected cells and the discarded supernatant shows that phage DNA entered the infected bacterial cell. The separation was not absolute, however. At least one of the two components, the viral nucleic acid or the viral coat protein, must have crossed into the host, and perhaps both did; in the event, a small fraction of phage protein entered the infected bacterial cell. That the nucleic acid was carried inside the host indicates that DNA is the molecule that carries hereditary information, even though a portion of the coat protein accompanied it into the cell, and notwithstanding the older conviction that the molecule was far too uniform to specify anything at all; in other words, hereditary information is transmitted by deoxyribonucleic acid.\n\nThat identification of the hereditary substance came first, and only afterwards was the architecture of the molecule itself put forward. Rosalind Franklin produced X-ray diffraction photographs of DNA fibres at King's College London. James Watson and Francis Crick proposed the double-helical structure of DNA in Nature in 1953.",
